  <h1>Specimen Types for Rapid Tests</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> The right specimen depends on the analyte and where it is found — blood for serology, swab for respiratory pathogens, urine for drugs and pregnancy, stool for GI pathogens.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Specimen</th><th>Typical tests</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Whole blood (WB)</th><td>Malaria, HIV, cardiac markers, H. pylori Ab</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Serum / Plasma (S/P)</th><td>Serology — HIV, HCV, HBsAg, syphilis, dengue IgG/IgM</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Nasal / NP swab</th><td>COVID-19, Influenza, RSV, Strep A</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Urine</th><td>Drug of abuse, pregnancy (hCG), LH, Legionella</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Feces</th><td>H. pylori Ag, rotavirus, adenovirus, norovirus, calprotectin, FOB</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Oral fluid</th><td>HIV, drug screening</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Specimen type is product-specific — each product page lists its accepted specimen.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>Which specimen should I choose?</strong> The specimen is determined by the analyte and product — each product page lists its accepted specimen.</p>
  </div>

